      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "c7fbfdea-d5e0-5651-a9d2-22ecca1b89a3",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Gabriela Mistral, Alan Sillitoe, Geoffrey Chaucer, Thomas Wyatt, Ivo Andrić, Saint-John Perse, Miguel Ángel Asturias, Pablo Neruda, George Seferis, Czesław Miłosz, Octavio Paz\n- Works: Saturday Night and Sunday Morning\n- Roles: poet-diplomats, angry young men\n- Awards: Nobel Prize in Literature, Author's Club First Novel Award\n\nFacts:\n- Alan Sillitoe was born on 4 March 1928 and died on 25 April 2010.\n- Alan Sillitoe was an English writer and was described as one of the \"angry young men\" of the 1950s.\n- Alan Sillitoe disliked the \"angry young men\" label, as did most of the other writers to whom it was applied.\n- Saturday Night and Sunday Morning was the first novel by British author Alan Sillitoe.\n- Saturday Night and Sunday Morning won the Author's Club First Novel Award.\n- In 1945, Gabriela Mistral became the first Latin American author to receive the Nobel Prize in Literature.\n- The Nobel Prize in Literature cited Gabriela Mistral \"for her lyric poetry which, inspired by powerful emotions, has made her name a symbol of the idealistic aspirations of the entire Latin American world\".\n- The best known poet-diplomats are perhaps Geoffrey Chaucer and Thomas Wyatt.\n- The category of poet-diplomats also includes Nobel Prize in Literature recipients Ivo Andrić, Gabriela Mistral, Saint-John Perse, Miguel Ángel Asturias, Pablo Neruda, George Seferis, Czesław Miłosz, and Octavio Paz.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
